    imageAlt:'Remote assistant preparing an auto repair estimate follow-up record',
    directAnswer:[
      'An auto repair shop can use a Philippines-based virtual assistant to organize customer contact preferences, vehicle and repair-order identifiers, technician findings supplied by the shop, estimate versions, approvals, declined items, and follow-up timing. The assistant may explain the administrative next step from approved wording, but should not diagnose a vehicle, recommend whether it is safe to drive, change labor or parts, promise completion, or record authorization that the customer did not clearly give.',
      'The safest starting lane begins after a shop owner or service advisor has released an estimate. The coordinator sends the approved version, records questions in the customer’s words, preserves each response, and routes technical, safety, price, warranty, or scope questions back to the advisor. An unanswered message, ambiguous reply, or request for a different repair is not approval.',
    ],
    sections:[
      {heading:'Anchor every follow-up to one estimate version',paragraphs:['Record the repair-order identifier, vehicle identifier used by the shop, estimate version, issue time, issuing advisor, included work, taxes or fees as displayed, and approved communication channel. Do not copy line items into a fresh document that can drift from the source. When a revised estimate appears, preserve the prior version and why it changed. The customer and shop need to know exactly which scope was presented when a response was received.']},
      {heading:'Preserve the difference between findings and decisions',paragraphs:['A technician’s recorded finding belongs to the shop’s technical record. The assistant can quote only language approved for the customer and should not translate it into a diagnosis, severity claim, or repair priority. If the customer asks whether the vehicle is safe, whether a repair is necessary, or what could happen if they wait, send the question to the service advisor. The remote coordinator did not inspect, test, drive, or disassemble the vehicle.']},
      {heading:'Require clear authorization evidence',paragraphs:['The shop defines accepted authorization channels and wording. Record who responded, when, through which verified contact, the exact estimate version, approved or declined items, stated limit, and any question or condition. Replies such as okay, go ahead with the important part, or call me later may be ambiguous. Do not convert silence, a read receipt, prior approval, or a spouse’s message into authorization. Hold work and ask the advisor to resolve unclear authority.']},
      {heading:'Keep price changes under advisor control',paragraphs:['Parts availability, added findings, taxes, labor, sublet work, shop supplies, and customer choices can change totals. The assistant should not recalculate, substitute a part, remove a line, apply a discount, or promise that a preliminary amount is final. Send only the owner-approved revision and identify what changed using supplied wording. If the customer disputes a charge or references another quote, preserve the comparison and route it without arguing value or criticizing another business.']},
      {heading:'Do not promise completion from a calendar',paragraphs:['An estimated completion time can depend on authorization, parts, technician capacity, testing, additional findings, and external vendors. Record the latest window and source, then use conditional wording approved by the shop. A bay schedule or shipped status does not prove completion. When the customer has transportation, work, childcare, or travel constraints, capture them for the advisor without guaranteeing accommodation. Only authorized staff releases the vehicle and confirms pickup conditions.']},
      {heading:'Use a work sample with changing scope',paragraphs:['Create fictional records for routine maintenance, a safety question, two estimate versions, backordered part, declined recommendation, unclear text approval, warranty question, added finding, disputed price, and completion delay. Ask the candidate to prepare follow-ups and status notes. Score version control, exact authorization, technical restraint, empathy without promises, and escalation. A candidate should leave some items waiting for the advisor rather than forcing every record into approved or declined.']},
      {heading:'Protect vehicle and customer records',paragraphs:['Provide access only to assigned repair orders, approved estimates, communication templates, limited scheduling views, and the advisor queue. Exclude payment-card data, refunds, pricing administration, parts purchasing, technician accounts, key-control records, broad exports, deletion, and account recovery. Use named accounts. Avoid copying license, address, insurance, or vehicle history into general tools. When a contact changes, follow the shop’s verification procedure before sending an estimate or discussing the job.']},
      {heading:'Create a shift handoff for open decisions',paragraphs:['List estimates sent, verified responses, ambiguous replies, advisor questions, parts or schedule updates supplied by owners, customer constraints, and the next approved contact time. Include estimate version and local shop timezone. Do not mark a message handled because it was forwarded. Name the advisor who accepted each technical, pricing, safety, or complaint item. The next coordinator should resume from source evidence instead of rereading private chat or guessing what a colored status means.']},
      {heading:'Audit rework, consent, and promise accuracy',paragraphs:['Review wrong estimate versions sent, authorization corrections, technical answers attempted, price promises corrected, duplicate contacts, missed opt-outs, and open questions without owners. Compare selected status records with communication evidence. Also review whether advisors answer quickly enough; delay can tempt customers and coordinators to treat a vague response as sufficient. Improve approval language and version displays when the same mistake repeats rather than relying on reminders to be careful.']},
      {heading:'Close the estimate with a traceable outcome',paragraphs:['Use approved outcomes such as fully authorized, partly authorized with listed items, declined, awaiting customer, revised by advisor, warranty review assigned, vehicle collected under owner procedure, or complaint transferred. Preserve the estimate and response that support the state. Do not say repair complete merely because an estimate closed, and do not erase declined work from the service history. A separate safety or customer complaint record may remain open after the commercial decision.']},
      {heading:'Write the brief around the service advisor',paragraphs:['Specify repair-order volume, covered locations, shop timezone, estimate source, approved contact channels, authorization rule, follow-up cadence, advisor response target, escalation categories, and end-of-shift report. Include redacted examples for revised, partial, ambiguous, delayed, and disputed estimates. Hire Assistant Near Me can recruit a Philippines-based assistant for remote follow-up. The shop keeps diagnosis, repair priority, safety advice, parts and labor choices, prices, warranties, work authorization interpretation, completion, payment, and vehicle release.']},
    ],
    sources:[
      {name:'FTC, Auto Repair Basics',url:'https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/auto-repair-basics',note:'Consumer context for estimates, authorization, maintenance, and repair records.'},
      {name:'NHTSA, Vehicle Safety Recalls',url:'https://www.nhtsa.gov/recalls',note:'Official recall lookup context; shop professionals handle case-specific advice.'},
    ],
